{
  "rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (often by ~6–15 EUR), especially from ~day 7 onward. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point miss: when history shows a peak and rollover, the model continues to project elevated/sideways levels instead of the subsequent sell-off. Add a regime check: after a local high + weakening last ~3–5 days, force a bearish drift for the next 1–3 weeks.",
    "Insufficient downside volatility: realized paths exhibit sharp step-down moves; the model smooths them and underestimates drawdowns. Increase downside convexity (fatter left tail) when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following high prints in history (80–84 area), forecasts remain anchored near the spike, while outcomes mean-revert quickly to low 70s/high 60s. Add faster mean reversion proportional to distance from a medium-term mean (e.g., 20–30d average).",
    "Horizon-specific error: near-term (days 1–3) errors are smaller; mid/long horizon (days 5–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-dependent correction: small adjustment for days 1–3, larger downward adjustment plus stronger mean reversion for days 5–30."
  ]
}
